Z||000000||LottaBallsCamaro||03-20-2001||10:59 PM||||Whose got it done right. Howd you do it? Also, what was the formula fiero?<P>------------------<BR>91'RS 305TBI no mods to date<BR>92' Lumina 275,000 miles and still humping!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||danny boy||03-20-2001||11:09 PM|||| Anyone with an 88 has far superior handling to anyone else.It is possible to swap an 88 rear cradle into an early car which I'll be doing as soon as I get around to ordering the strut tower adapters and held Motorsports also has a completely revised front suspension.<BR> Formulas were an 88 only option. they basically look like a coupe with a GT spoiler and wheels.They all had V6s in them. Pretty much like a Firebird Formula(I think). All go, little show. Stripper model GT.<BR>     Hey, at least you're not the stupid GTO guy.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stealth_feb2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||LottaBallsCamaro||03-20-2001||11:13 PM||||What is the difference between a 88 and everything else(I know it was a revised suspension)||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||03-20-2001||11:21 PM||||The suspension was a clean sheet approach (on the drawing boards in '83 GRRRRR!!!) with multilink suspension in the back as opposed to the A-arm and ball joint, the struts were angled a little differently and in the front was a different shock set up with improved A-arms that wouldn't flex as much as the 84-87 would. Different spring rates and shock tuning all the way around - for the first time...a stock sway bar for the back end.<P>Sigh. Oh why didn't they just pop the money for it in 84.....<P>I know Im missing something, I just can't think of it...but no fear, someone else will mention it!||||reg||9
Z||000004||gixxer||03-20-2001||11:58 PM||||Technically the '88 rear is called a Tri-link.<BR>Two lower lateral links per side, and a MacPherson strut. A single fore/aft link each side to take most of the accelerative, braking and some of the bump impact loads. This is a VERY good rear suspension by anybody's standards.<BR>The front end had to be completely revised to shorten the spindle length, which reduces steering wheel feedback from the wheel/tire assembly speeding up and slowing down as it passes over bumps. As good a reason as any to put smaller. lighter tires on the front. Weird, but true..<BR>All Fieros suffer from a shortage of suspension travel front and rear, which makes lowering them and retaining some semblance of handling on rough roads difficult. Here, attention to bump stop length, shape and shock quality will pay off.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||03-21-2001||12:08 AM||||The handling on my 86 is more "race". It has poly bushings, stiff springs, front and rear larger sway bars, and KYB shocks/struts. It handles better than a stock 88 BUT there is a catch, it rides very stiff. (kidney belt and neck brace rquired)The 88 suspension is configured simular to a Lotus set-up.<BR>(NO!!! It was NOT designed by Lotus as the idiots on Ebay would have you believe!)<P>The earlier suspensions also have a "bump steer" problem But there are kits to correct it. If you plan on racing SCCA or road course and plan on lots of mods, The 84-87 suspension would save you some money. The 88's cost more to mod due to cost of parts, but the initial design is better and 88's have vented brake rotors as opposed to the solid rotors of the 84-87 Fiero's.<P>There are even kits that use super strong control arms and adjustable coil-over suspensions. Raynes use to make them but now Held motorsports has them.<P>Basically how much do you want to spend? 88 vehicles are also higher in cost. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||03-21-2001||08:10 AM||||Installing a rear sway bar is the most cost effective suspension upgrade for the pre-88s.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||03-21-2001||09:21 AM||||-polyurathe everywhere dog bone too<BR>-ground control coil overs with eibach<BR> springs<BR> 375lbs<BR>-koni struts rear full hard<BR>-Koni shocks up front on 2nd setting<BR>-Carrera springs up front<BR>-Big bars front and rear (26mm? I will<BR> measure)<BR>-255/40/17 Pirelli PZero's (1.9 Degrees camber)<BR>-225/45/17's front<BR>-moog problem solver ball joints<BR>-Tr Motorsports typhoons (wheels)<P>Stoppers:<BR>11.26" vented discs on all 4 corners , wilwood 4 piston dynalite calipers up front , cadillac calipers in rear , Blazer master Cylinder.<P>Needs big ass motor.<P>people ask how low it is , you can't fit a coke can under the side of the car , I have demolished 2 sets of rear sway bar bushings when I pulled into 7-11 and hit the tank caps.<P>best set up is not an 88. best setup I have seen (heard about from lots of fiero autocrossers/ racers) is a 84 to 87 chassis , 88 rear and tubular front.<P>Tubular front is my next project. Not going to swap to 88 rear because I hope to have a N* put into the car when I grad in a year and 88 rear is uncharted territory for a N* conversion. I may just get a bump steer kit.<P>My car is a god example of a street autocross car , but it rides very very hard , even on full soft , the car rides hard. But it tracks like a go kart.<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000008||pherder||03-21-2001||10:40 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>There are even kits that use super strong control arms and adjustable coil-over suspensions. RCC use to make them but now Held motorsports has them.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>First of all, RCC STILL sells 84-87 suspension pieces.  Held Motorsports purchased Ryane Motorsports designs/tooling.<P>Second, Well I hope when I am done, I will have one of the better suspensions on one of my 88 Fieros (probably not the best, there is always room for improvement!)<P>Ryane Motorsports tubular control arms (upper and lower) with coil over shocks, Poly bushings EVERYWHERE!  NEW but stock brake assemblies (it's an 88, they work <B>good</B> stock!) Tubular rear suspension pieces (poly bushings, of course) Adjustable height coil over struts.  16x7 wheels all around with Dunlop 8000 series 225R50 15 tires.  Sway bars with poly bushings both front and rear.<P>Now I just got to assemble it all in one of the 88s.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul<BR>------------------<BR>88 Formula<BR>88 Coupe<BR>86 SE<BR>95 GMC<BR>92 Mazda<BR>55 Jones<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135<BR>pherder@kscable.com<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 03-21-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000009||Oreif||03-21-2001||10:45 AM||||pherder, Thanks for the correction. I edited my post.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula88||03-21-2001||10:56 AM||||Don't forget, in addition to the revised front and rear suspension, the brakes were changed as well in 88.  The 88's have vented discs instead of solid.  Also, the offset on the front wheels is different because the steering rack on the 88's is 1" wider than previous years.  So the '88's also have different offset wheels.  And yes, the Formula is mechanically identical to the GT, same drivetrain and suspension, but is built with the lighter coupe body and typically has fewer options.||||reg||3
Z||000011||Will||03-21-2001||01:23 PM||||I have an 86 SE w/ transplanted '88 front suspension. I've noticed some decent push at extremely low speeds which isn't present on my '87. Both cars are neutral to slight oversteer at higher speeds.<P>On my '87 I have aluminum cradle bushings and a front WS6 bar on the rear. The front suspension is almost stock. I'm going to poly control arm bushings and battery relocation shortly. I have rod-end sway bar links on the front, and will be puttign them on teh rear this weekend.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Greg Piet||03-21-2001||02:51 PM||||I continually hear that the 88 suspension is far superior...  <BR>I am really curious by how much.<BR>From my understanding the skidpad is the most typical test of suspension.<P>SO...   How is it on the skidpad?<BR>I know my 85 V6 pulled .84g bone stock.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">  <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||03-23-2001||12:38 AM||||Hey Will,how do the rod ends on the front bar work,I mean,did it make a difference in the ride as opposed to the stock type links?<P>I have progressive rate front springs(max 305#)made by moog,along with a dropped spindle effect(taller lower balljoints)that rides and handles pretty good.Of course anything can be made to work better <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||hugh||03-23-2001||01:45 AM||||Paul,I am in the prosess of putting Ryane's front end on my 88GT.The steel sleeves that fit inside the poly bushings on the upper control arms were too short and would squeeze the arms together when tightened.They needed to be 1.75 inch(the length of the bushing),but were only 1.63 in.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Will||03-23-2001||02:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>Hey Will,how do the rod ends on the front bar work,I mean,did it make a difference in the ride as opposed to the stock type links?<P>I have progressive rate front springs(max 305#)made by moog,along with a dropped spindle effect(taller lower balljoints)that rides and handles pretty good.Of course anything can be made to work better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>No increase in ride harshness (At least none that I felt).<BR>The difference is that the car now exhibits transient understeer on rapid turn-in. This is because the front bar "catches" before the rear bar, momentarily giving the front much more roll stiffness than the rear.<BR>It should be back to neutral when I put the rears on tomorrow.<BR>The front end rolls a bit less. I suspect I'll see a bigger improvement from the rear than from the front.||||reg||1
Z||000016||lowCG||03-23-2001||07:29 PM||||My rear bar has rod ends,and I do notice a more prompt response compared to the front,think I'll be doing the front next,any helpful tips you can think of?<BR>Let us know how the car works with the rear end connected like the front.||||reg||1
Z||000017||pherder||03-23-2001||07:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>Paul,I am in the prosess of putting Ryane's front end on my 88GT.The steel sleeves that fit inside the poly bushings on the upper control arms were too short and would squeeze the arms together when tightened.They needed to be 1.75 inch(the length of the bushing),but were only 1.63 in.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hugh,<P><B>GOOD</B> information!!!  Since I haven't install it yet, I will take measurements and make sure it is right!<P>BTW, I got the replacement mounting bar from the Fiero Store after spending a <B>LOT</B> of time trying to get one of the original ones off a stock upper a-arm!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Paul<P>||||reg||1
